      "writtenOn": "2017-06-29T03:39:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I feel like the meaning of spare capacity here can be refined to also consider the maximum capacity of the cluster. For example, say the big cluster and little cluster both have a cur_capacity of 500. But the max capacity (capacity_orig_of) will be different. \n\nSay the max capacity of the BIG is 1024 and the max capacity of the little is 800. Then the spare will be 524 on the big and 300 on the little. In this case we may not be comparing spare capacity correctly. So IMO the definition of \u0027spare\u0027 can be refined as: max capacity of CPU - cur_capacity.\n\nFurther I feel, shouldn\u0027t the \"spare capacity\" more correctly be actually:\n(max capacity - new_util)?\n\nI feel calculation of spare capacity shouldn\u0027t consider cur_capacity because the OPP will change anyway once new_util comes into effect. So spare_capacity \u003d (max_capacity - new_util) sounds more accurate to me. What do you say?",
      "revId": "a231d004f4bd5077c28fe349df42d90fa2ff7658",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "57bb01d3_c62989c7",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/fair.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6296,
      "author": {
        "id": 1119068
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-06-29T10:11:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In principle I agree with you, however I have the impression we are chasing two different optimization goals here.\n\nDo we wanna to \"improve speedup margins\" or \"reduce latency\"?\n\nWhat you propose here seems like selecting a CPU to \"improve the potential speedup\":\n\n  IF the task fits in the current capacity\n  THEN opt for the CPU with the bigger _potential_ capacity\n       (i.e. capacity_orig_of)\n\nHowever, here we are selecting a target for a \"prefer_idle\" task, i.e. a latency sensitive task. If we consider latency reduction as our main goal, the a policy like:\n\n  IF the task fits in the current capacity\n  THEN opt for the CPU with the bigger _current_ capacity\n       (i.e. capacity_curr_of)\n\nseems to be a sensible choice too.\n\nHowever, such a policy would prefer a LITTLE cpu in the scenario you depicted. Moreover, we can argue that by picking the CPU with the maximum _current_ spare capacity, we increase the chances to put the task on a CPU where other tasks are likely to be scheduler (just because we have spare capacity)?\n\nDon\u0027t really know which policy is really the best, we should experiment a bit to find out.\n\nAs a final comment, I think this topic is already in the domain of the improvements. I cannot see in the original code any attempt to use capacity_orig_of for placement of prefer_idle tasks on a system without IDLE cpus.\nThus, we should probably start by convincing our self if the current implementation is a good enough starting point and merge it. This will make definitively more simple to experiment on devices with small modifications/updates on top of a more clean and easy to understand code-base.",
      "parentUuid": "3de3a6c2_4a649d74",
      "revId": "a231d004f4bd5077c28fe349df42d90fa2ff7658",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5624ee17_237ce492",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/fair.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6296,
